A||||13||Andre||Weird 87 GT Engine Problem - Ideas??||||1||
Z||000000||Andre||10-08-2001||11:20 PM||||The problem:  Engine lacks power (about 80% of normal) and has a "whistle noise" (almost like a turbo spooling up) under acceleration.  The sound seems to come from the intake manifold.<P>Other details: Starts normal, idles perfectly at 900 rpm, plugs all perfect color.<P>Already checked: EGR (blocked off), vacuum lines; intake gaskets, plugs, throttle body cleaned.<P>Recent changes just before the problem appeared:  Voltage breakthrough on coil, replaced with MSD Coil, electronic spark module in the distributor failed as a result - replaced with new GM module.<P>Any ideas on what else to look/check for?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rick 88||10-08-2001||11:31 PM||||The whistle noise could be a slight vacuum leak you need to find. Have you checked your inital timing? If it is retarded you will be down on power.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000002||Andre||10-08-2001||11:36 PM||||Thanks Rick88, but I forgot to mention that the timing is spot on at 10 degrees.||||reg||1
Z||000003||crgkstnr||10-09-2001||10:39 AM||||Andre,<BR>A partly plugged catalytic converter can whistle and rob power.  <BR>Craig<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||redfiero||10-09-2001||11:04 AM||||I had the same problem with a 280ZX that I had.  The converter was clogged.  Over the course of a few weeks, the power dropped incrementally.<P>Good Luck,<BR>matt||||reg||1
Z||000005||Andre||10-13-2001||06:14 PM||||Thanks Craig and Matt  - You guys ROCK!<P>I did not suspect the catalytic converter, since we installed a new so-called "high performance" cat about 18 months ago.  I took it off this morning, and the "brick" is more than 50% blocked and starting to disintegrate.  So much for the crap they sell you at the local parts store.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Andre||10-13-2001||06:31 PM||||Thanks Craig and Matt - You guys ROCK!<P>I never suspected the catalytic converter, since we installed a new so-called "high performance" cat about 18 months ago.  I took it off this morning, and the internal "brick" is about 50% blocked and in the process of disintegrating.  So much for product quality from your local parts store.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||10-13-2001||06:41 PM||||My '86SE v-6 4-speed has a whistle/whine under harder acceleration. It never has any performance issues, but I think it is the coolest sound. People ride in it and ask what is it, I tell them I have no idea but I love it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SKIDMARK||10-13-2001||10:12 PM||||The converter won't just plug itself. There has to be another problem causing carbon to accumulate in the converter. Look for vacuum leaks and other fuel delivery problems after you get it running again. You may just end up plugging up your new converter if you don't. <P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000009||GarethWright||10-14-2001||12:19 AM||||Could the extra emissions from having the EGR plugged clog the cat faster?  No idea myself, just thinkin aloud to the guys who might know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||GarethWright||10-14-2001||12:19 AM||||Could the extra emissions from having the EGR plugged clog the cat faster?  No idea myself, just thinkin aloud to the guys who might know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cozmo||10-14-2001||04:29 AM||||Yeah, the computer expects to see a functional EGR.  You might want to check the O2 sensor, if it's gone stupid the car can run rich and plug up the cat and in turn the muffler also.<P>BobC||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||10-14-2001||12:59 PM||||Yeah, my Formula had a 2 year old cat plug up.  It also had a bad EGR tube, and I'm gonna replace the O2 since I have no idea how old it is.  Bottom line, a converter shouldn't plug up, so something is causing it.  Fix that problem or you'll keep clogging converters.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Bazooka||10-14-2001||03:40 PM||||After you replace the Cat, also check th 02 sensor and the EGR Valve. The 02 may have been contaminated by the same stuff as your Cat (improper fuel type maybe). The element of the 02 sensor should be a light tawny color from visual inspection, but is best checked with a scanner (F.I) or a DVOM (Carbureted). The EGR is also critical for smooth power so its best to pull it off and clean the pintle and passage ducts in the intake.<BR>**************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SKIDMARK:<BR><B>The converter won't just plug itself. There has to be another problem causing carbon to accumulate in the converter. Look for vacuum leaks and other fuel delivery problems after you get it running again. You may just end up plugging up your new converter if you don't. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>********************************<BR>||||reg||1
